{
  "abstract": "We study graphs formed from a common root, disjoint induced seven-vertex-critical cores, and arbitrary edges between the cores. Adding two adjacent universal apices makes rooted seven-colorability a necessary obstruction to a ten-chromatic biplanar construction. We prove an unbounded coloring theorem when the root misses between two and four vertices in each core, each covered core is six-clique-free, and every union of cores satisfies the Euler-derived weighted edge budget. No degeneracy assumption on the interface multigraph is needed. A sharp palette lemma localizes every obstruction to one heavy pair, and structural recoloring repairs all possible pairs. With up to five omissions per core, we prove a fixed-color extension theorem that also permits an arbitrary root-covered private remainder, retaining all its edges. Every seven-coloring of a piece support with at most one unused unit of the weighted budget extends while preserving every original color. The proof selects independent root-colored vertices, localizes residual list obstructions to a six-plus-one palette clique or seven singleton palettes, and repairs and absorbs each support. A stronger uniform budget gives unconditional coloring. Root-sensitive two-carrier and six-target-star lemmas justify the repairs, including fixed root-colored boundary contacts and rigid singleton pieces. Read-only standard-library checkers verify local controls and nine literal mixed networks; computation is not a proof premise. These results close the common-root four-omission family and reduce the five-omission family to a precise dense-support coloring question. They do not resolve unrestricted Earth–Moon.",
